            "\n",
            "\\subsection{Effectiveness of the simple heuristics}\\label{appendix:rule}\n",
            "In Section~\\ref{sec:proposal}, we apply simple heuristics to encourage the model to use simple and accurate demonstrations. Similar to the criteria of the hand-crafting demonstrations in \\citet{cot_wei}, our selection criteria follow simple heuristics to encourage sampling simpler questions and rationales: set the selected demonstration $d^{(i)}$ as $d_j^{(i)}$ if it has a question $q_j^{(i)}$ with no more than $60$ tokens and a rationale $r_j^{(i)}$ with no more than $5$ reasoning steps.\\footnote{Because Zero-Shot-CoT often uses ``$\\backslash$n'' for separating the reasoning steps, the rule can be easily implemented by counting the ``$\\backslash$n'' tokens in the generated rationales.} For arithmetic reasoning tasks except for AQuA (because it is a multiple-choice problem), we require that $a_j^{(i)}$ is not empty and appears in $r_j^{(i)}$\\footnote{In arithmetic reasoning tasks, rationales often infer their answers at the last few tokens, as demonstrations examples shown in Appendix \\ref{sec:appendix-full-prompts}.} to mitigate the risk of rationale-answer mismatches (as we find that such mistakes are harmful in Appendix \\ref{appendix:demo_impact}). If the question, rationale, and the answer satisfy the conditions above, then a candidate demonstration $d_j^{(i)}$ for the $i$-th cluster is constructed by concatenating the question, rationale, and answer: $[\\text{Q: } q_j^{(i)}, \\text{A: } r_j^{(i)} \\circ a_j^{(i)}]$.\n",
            "\n",
